LEAD = {
    "headline": "A THOUSAND DOLLARS BOUGHT 32.4 MILLION VIEWS FOR ONE SONG, BECAUSE THE PAYOUTS WERE CAPPED.  AT THE PUBLISHED RATE THAT SHOULD HAVE COST THIRTY TIMES MORE",
    "deck": "Twitch's chief executive spent last week publicly embracing the clip economy, and Tubefilter asked yesterday whether clips are why the platform's viewership is up.  Nobody has proved that.  But underneath the question sits a distribution market with a published rate card, and almost nobody on the brand side has read it properly.",
    "stamps": [
        ("TUBEFILTER · 28 SEP", "https://www.tubefilter.com/2026/09/28/twitch-clipping-impact-on-traffic-dan-clancy/"),
        ("GAMESBEAT · 24 SEP", "https://gamesbeat.com/twitch-is-embracing-the-clip-economy-but-wants-viewers-to-stick-around-for-the-livestream/"),
        ("VARIETY · 26 MAR", "https://variety.com/2026/music/news/clipping-marketing-tool-took-over-music-industry-1236699705/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Start with the news, because it is small and the thing underneath it is not.  James Hale at Tubefilter ran a piece last night headlined with a question: Twitch's viewership is up year over year, are clips the reason?  The number in it is 1.56 billion hours watched in August, Twitch's strongest month of 2026, up about 7 percent on last year.  That figure is analyst Zach Bussey reading Streams Charts data, not Twitch.  Dan Clancy, who runs Twitch, attributed no viewership number to clipping at all.  So treat the causation as an open question that a trade publication raised, not as a finding.",
        "What Clancy did say, to Alexander Lee at GamesBeat at the Tokyo Game Show last Thursday, is the part worth keeping.  <mark>\"It's because these feeds are now more and more being dominated with AI, and you don't know what's real and what's not.  When you see a Twitch clip, it's kind of messy and noisy, and you know it's not AI.  You kind of look at it, and you know this really happened.\"</mark>  That is a chief executive arguing that low production value has become a credibility signal.  Hold that thought, because it collides with roughly every brand video brief written this year.",
        "Now the market he is describing, which has a price list.  Steven Horowitz laid it out in Variety in March and the mechanics have not changed since.  Clippers — independent contractors who cut short vertical pieces out of long-form video and post them across accounts they control — are paid <mark>one to five dollars for every thousand impressions, until they hit a predetermined cap.</mark>  Whole campaigns run, on average, between one and five thousand dollars.  The marketplace most of this runs through, Whop, raised 200 million dollars from Tether last month at a 1.6 billion dollar valuation.",
        "Here is the campaign that should change how you price this.  <mark>Electronic musician John Summit's single Lights Go Out had an initial budget of 1,050 dollars across eight days in January.  It produced 32.4 million views and 1.4 million likes, across 29 approved clips from 13 different creators.  The top clip did 5.3 million views on its own.</mark>  Run the arithmetic yourself — ours — and 32.4 million views at the bottom of that published rate should have cost about 32,400 dollars.  It cost 1,050.  That is roughly three cents per thousand views, against a rate card that starts at a dollar.",
        "The cap is the whole mechanism, and it is the thing nobody explains.  You are not buying impressions.  You are buying a bounded number of attempts, and each clipper's earnings stop at a ceiling while the clip's reach does not.  A brand that budgets clipping as views multiplied by rate has misunderstood the instrument it is buying.  The spend is fixed and the outcome is a lottery with a lot of tickets.",
        "Then look at what actually travelled, because this is where brand video gets its warning.  Summit's two biggest clips both had the song playing underneath footage of Steph Curry and Michael Jordan on a basketball court.  The viewer who stopped got the product — the song was the thing being sold and the thing being delivered, in the same three seconds, with nothing asked of them.  A brand clip is almost never that.  It is a reference to a product that lives somewhere else, and it asks the viewer to go and get it.  Same rate card, opposite economics.",
        "The honest risks, in order of how quickly they will land on you.  The Federal Trade Commission has disclosure rules for creators and, per Variety, no equivalent parameters for clipping, and it declined to comment; agencies are openly describing that gap as an advantage, which is exactly the sort of advantage that closes with a press release.  Instagram announced on 30 April that accounts regularly reposting content they did not create lose recommendation eligibility.  And Sam Alavi, who manages Bbno$ and has run these campaigns since 2024, says the quiet part himself: \"the value of the view is going down — you can ask any brand.\"",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("$1,050", "initial budget on the John Summit clipping campaign, over eight days in January"),
        ("32.4M", "views it produced, across 29 approved clips from 13 creators"),
        ("$1-$5", "what a clipper earns per thousand impressions, until a capped ceiling"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "The Variety reporting is six months old and is used here for the rate card and the Summit campaign, not as news; the news peg is yesterday's Tubefilter piece and last Thursday's GamesBeat interview.  The Summit figures are Whop's, via Variety, and no independent measurement company has confirmed them.  The three cents per thousand and the 32,400 dollar comparison are our arithmetic on Variety's published numbers, not figures anybody reported.  Whop sells the marketplace, and Whop's chief executive is the source for the claims about how well it works.",
    "so_what": "Every brand sitting on long-form video already owns the raw material for this market and is the only party not selling into it.  But the reason it works for a song is that the clip hands over the product itself, and the reason it mostly will not work for you is that your clip is a signpost.  The cap is the other half — it means the downside is genuinely small, which makes this worth one honest test rather than a line on next year's plan.",
    "do_this": "Run one capped clipping test this quarter on a piece of long-form you already own, budget it at the cost of a single creator post, and judge it on saves, comments and branded search rather than views — then write a disclosure requirement into the brief before you commission a single clip.",
}

